#import "../../lib/24xx.typ": *

#show: xx.with(
  title: "AX&X: High Level Play",
  subtitle: "Multiple attacks and the veteran's blade. A high-level supplement for AX&X.",
  cols: 2,
)

= The Veteran's Many Blows

The core runs every fighter on one attack a round until the Warrior tracks earn more: a second at Level 6, a third at 11. This supplement is what those extra blows mean at the table, and every other way a character comes to strike more than once in a round.

It adds no new dice. Each blow is still #k[1d20 + your Attack Mod] against AC. Where the core sets when the attacks arrive, this sets how they are spent. Take the progression and leave the options, or the reverse.

= The Warrior's Progression

- #item[When][A core #k[Warrior], Fighter, Barbarian, or Ranger, strikes twice from Level 6 and three times from Level 11. The core sets this and keeps it. No other track gains extra attacks from Level.]
- #item[Melee Only][The extra blows are melee. A bow, sling, or crossbow holds its own rate of fire whatever your Level, see Missiles below. Level makes an archer hit harder, not oftener.]
- #item[Spending Them][Each attack is a full roll at the same Attack Mod. Split them across any foes in reach, named before you roll. A short step may fall between blows, but a full move is the alternative to attacking, not a part of it.]
- #item[Charge and Withdraw][A Charge lands your full attacks, all on the foe you closed with, at +2 and -2 to your own AC until your next turn. A Withdraw spends the action and draws no blows of its own.]
- #item[The Ceiling][Three attacks is the top of the base rule. For a long campaign, allow a fourth at Level 16. Weapon Mastery is the other road past three.]

= Variant: The Old Cadence

For the grittier first-edition curve, replace the whole-attack jumps with fractions. A Warrior strikes once a round through Level 6, then #k[three times in two rounds], an extra blow every other round, from 7 to 12, then #k[twice a round] from 13. Mark the off rounds to track the half. It bookkeeps more; the core's clean 6-and-11 is the default.

= Mowing the Rabble

- #item[Sweep][Against foes of #k[1 HD or less], a Warrior makes one melee attack per Level, each a full roll, striking as many as stand in reach. This is how one veteran scatters a mob of goblins. Nothing above 1 HD falls to it.]
- #item[Cleave][When any melee attack drops a foe, the swing carries to another within reach, one free blow, once a turn. A Warrior at full Sweep has no need of it; a lone duelist does.]

= More Hands, More Blows

- #item[Two Weapons][A light weapon in the off hand buys one extra melee attack a round, at -2 to both rolls, and no shield. The off-hand blow adds no STR to its damage. Open to any Level.]
- #item[Weapon Mastery][Optional. A Fighter may master a single weapon: #k[+1 to hit and +1 damage] with it, and a half-attack on its line, three blows in two rounds at Level 1, rising with the progression toward five in two at the ceiling. A second mastered weapon costs a Level's training in play.]

= Missiles Keep Their Own Rate

- #item[Rate of Fire][Level never multiplies missiles. At every Level a bow looses twice a round, a sling once, darts three times, a crossbow once and then a reload. High DEX and Level sharpen the aim, not the count.]
- #item[The Exceptions][Only the weapon's own nature changes this. A master archer gains the half-attack with that bow, and point-blank range or a braced volley follow the Combat supplement, not Level.]

= The Other Tracks

- #item[Specialist and Priest][The #k[+half Level] to hit is their whole martial growth, with no extra attacks from Level. A Thief's edge stays the Backstab; a Cleric's, the spell and the Turn.]
- #item[Mage][Never gains an extra attack. The second spell, not the second swing, is the Mage's volume.]
- #item[Monsters][A high-HD monster already strikes at + HD and often several times a round, claw, claw, bite. That is its progression. A lone Warrior's Sweep is what keeps him even with a horde, his second and third blows what keep him in a duel with a giant.]

= Spending the Surplus

Attacks to spare are attacks to gamble. In place of one of your blows, attempt a masterstroke, resolved as the Combat supplement sets out, by a STR or opposed check, or a save for the target. Roll or pick.

#rolltable("1d6", "Masterstrokes", note: [Each spends one attack of a multi-attack round. A plain fighter may try one too, but a veteran can afford to miss.], (
  item[Disarm][An attack against the foe's guard, or opposed STR; win and the weapon flies 10 ft.],
  item[Trip][STR against the foe's STR or HD; it falls Prone.],
  item[Push][Drive a foe back 5 ft, off a ledge or out of a door it held.],
  item[Pin][On a grabbed or Helpless foe, lock it fast; it acts at -4 until it tears free.],
  item[Sunder][Shatter a shield or a readied weapon in place of a wound.],
  item[Aimed Blow][Strike at -4 for a precise hit: a called limb, a gap in the plate, the hand that holds the key.],
))
